From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Dariusz Majnert <dariusz.majnert@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 14 Aug 2026 18:32:49 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] native automatic tool wear

Ported to v2.10.0-pre2. Differences from the patch on the old base:
upstream 7180d28698 made #5401-#5409 the loaded tool's stored offset
again and added #5081-#5089 for the applied offset, so #5401-#5409 now
hold stored geometry (refreshed by M6, M61, startup and G10 L1/L3/L10/
L11), #5430-#5438 stored wear, and #5081-#5089 the applied geometry plus
wear written by the automatic M6/M61 activation, G43, G43 H, G43.1,
G43.2 and G43.4 and zeroed by G49. G43.4 shares G43's path and so also
applies geometry plus wear; the automatic activation re-applies as G43.4
while the offset in effect is G43.4's, so a later G49 still undoes its
kinematics switch (upstream's RETAIN_G43, extended for G43.4, is still
removed). Wear edits and composition use upstream's per-axis A-W unit
conversion. The pybind11 gcode module accepts 23-item tool entries
(geometry plus wear, as linuxcnc.stat.tool_table now returns) besides
the 14-item ones, so the preview applies the same offset as the machine;
before, a full entry failed the parse. The preview's lathe tool shape
reads diameter, angles and orientation by position, not as the last
four fields. The tool-table default moves into upstream's
CANON_TOOL_TABLE_INIT, and the deleted EMC_TOOL_STAT::operator= is not
restored. tests/tool-wear-task covers the task path on a lathe, the
db-g10-offset test covers G10 L3 notifying a v2.2 db program, and a
renderer test covers wear in the preview.

Fixes from review of the port:

- The tool and its automatic offset take effect together. Canon attaches
  an offset commanded right behind a tool change (the automatic
  activation, or a G43/G49/G43.2 in the same block) to that
  EMC_TOOL_LOAD or EMC_TOOL_SET_NUMBER, and task applies it to motion
  and to stat.tool_offset in the cycle the tool takes effect; an aborted
  change drops it. CHANGE_TOOL_NUMBER flushes buffered motion first.
  emcTaskAbort() and the error paths that clear the interpreter list
  reset canon's offset from task status, and Interp::synch() reads the
  interpreter's offset back from canon. Before, an abort or E-stop
  around a pending change could leave the offset of one tool applied
  with another tool loaded.
- M61 with cutter radius compensation on is refused before any state
  changes, as M6 is.
- A tool line may hold at most 510 characters before its newline.
  Loading a table, reading an entry, reading a database reply,
  formatting, saving and notifying a database program refuse a longer
  line with an error instead of cutting a number short; the formatter no
  longer overruns its buffer. A table loaded with refused lines is not
  saved over until it is reloaded without them.
- A tool edit whose save or database notification fails is rolled back
  in memory.
- GET_EXTERNAL_TOOL_TABLE returns an initialized entry for an
  out-of-range index.
- The preview clears its stored tool offset between parses.
- New tests: tool-wear-abort, tool-wear-restart, interp/tool-line-length
  and tooldb/db-line-length, with more cases in the existing ones. The
  G-code documentation states that G10 L10/L11 set geometry without
  regard to stored wear and that nothing is applied after a restart
  until G43, M6 or M61.
